d I just  really want to demo it quickly all right  so databases at the risk of  oversimplifying you can think of  databases as being in one of two  categories there's relational databases  and then there's the newer no sequel or  object-oriented databases we'll focus on  the first one and that's mostly for  historical reasons they've been around  for longer the infrastructure around  them in general not just an AR is  stronger and very quickly relational  databases and their management systems  store data in columns and rows these  make up tables  it looks spreadsheet II and they use SQL  which is a language for querying data a  few popular databases that are  relational or in this case open source  are my sequel Postgres and sequel Lite  recently for distributed systems you  also have things like Apache hive and  fodder Impala which are relational like  and most of these packages work really  well with relational databases there's  nothing stopping them from working with  the no sequel databases there needs to  be more infrastructure there and that we  haven't gone into it yet so I'm going to  skip over this a little bit and get  right into the packages so the first  foundational package that we're  interested in when it comes to databases  in our it's called vbi and it stands for  database interface and the whole idea of  vbi is that it's a standard it's an  interface that defines how you should  interact with the database from our so  basically it as s3 generics which are  functions that then are implemented for  particular types of connections so dbi  itself doesn't care what type of  connection you have it just says that it  whatever it is your connection you have  to implement this method for connecting  that method for disconnecting  and a bunch of other methods so the  advantage of this is that you get  different databases using basically the  same language and that gives you a lot  of portability in your code you can  basically migrate your database to  another system and be able to use a lot  of your old code so one thing that I  want to note particularly is that as I  said DVI itself doesn't care what is the  particular database you're using all of  that work is done by other packages and  these packages will implement all of  these methods for a particular database  or a database management system so these  are the so-called vbi compliance  backends our sequel Lite or my sequel  our Postgres are all examples of vbi  compliance packages and so you need to  use both of them if you want to connect  to I can see qualité database and so and  if this sounds like extra work to you  again the advantage is that by having  vbi you have a very portable language  for a lot of different packages in  practice a vbi looks like this you can  create a connection by calling VV  connect in this case I am creating a  connection to a sequel Lite database in  memory you can write a table you can get  a query this is SQL right here so it you  have to be a little bit fluent in SQL  and when you're done is you need to  disconnect your connection which is very  important because if you forget to do  this and you're doing a lot of queries  and creating a lot of connections you  will actually leak a lot of connections  so the gist of it's pretty simple one  thing that I want to note in DVI before  we move on is that it also provides  really good sequel injection prevention  function so a sequel injection is when  users third parties can go to your app  or go to your program and inject code  that has basically malicious intents so  in this case even though the string here  is asking for a name a user can do this  and and if you're not escaping this  correctly this can cause your database  to lose a whole table so this has been a  problem ever since relational databases  have existed and the whole point of here  the punchline as this comic is that you  need to sanitize your database inputs  and vbi has a lot of functions for that  so keep that in mind that is important  so moving on to ODBC I'm going to spend  very little time on this ODBC a is yet  another standard yet another  specification so ODBC unlike DVI however  is independent of operating system  programming language and it really is  just a standard API of how to interact  with databases how this relates to our  is that very recently we have the ODBC  package ODBC now in lower case which is  a DB I compliance back-end so what this  means is that if you have an ODBC driver  for a package that has been written by  whomever that is not an R because ODBC  is not language dependent and then you  have the VI in ODBC you don't need to  write your own driver for whatever  database you want to connect to if you  already have the ODBC driver so that's  the real power of this any really  augments the power of DB I by sending it  to so many other databases that didn't  have specific drivers in practice it  looks very similar to what we've seen  already you have DB connect you have a  series of arguments one thing to note is  that since you're always connecting with  ODBC these arguments are actually the  same always now that it's always UID for  example instead of user  or username or one of the thousand  variants that you can have and then you  can continue doing doing a very similar  job disconnecting at the end the plier  so the idea now that you know about DVI  and you can use DVI for so many things  the idea to take it one level up is to  not have to worry about sequel and just  use the plier syntax use our code to  talk to a database to extract data to  insert data to transform data so the  plier extends and wraps a lot of DVI  methods and you can do most of what you  can do what you can do with the VI in  sequel directly we'd apply NR there are  a few things that you feel me dbi for  because the plier is not magic although  it seems like it's sometimes and there  have been actually a very recent revamp  of the plier and the introduction of a  new package called DB plier so this is a  fresh off the presses and the bottom  line I think is if you have if you're  used to R and if you're used to the deep  layer verbs like filter and mutation and  group by and summarize it's a very good  idea to go with apply it also does a  bunch of optimizations for you with  sequel translation and lazy evaluation  so you never loading data into our until  you need it so if the player is good  enough for you it tends to be a great  idea to use it and I hope this slide  makes it really clear weighted so as you  can see we have exactly the same  standard for connection that we have  with EBI but now to query it we are just  using the apply year we're piping things  it's very easy for us to read as human  beings we have a connection you asked  for the table called city and any ask  for the first two rows and that's it  it's very intuitive there's no select  asterisk from city limit to that we had  to deal with before  so finally pool so after all these  packages why do we need another one and  the problem that pool tries to solve is  how what is the best way to interact  with the database from shiny so shiny  brings its own set of constraints  therefore every one session there's only  a single our process and potentially  many users establishing connections to a  remote database can take quite a while  and they can go down at any time because  of network problems or a bunch of other  things so you're really in this  conundrum that you don't want a fresh  connection for every query that you make  for every user action because I would be  so slow but you also don't want one  connection for app because what if it  breaks down what if there's two users  trying to make a query at the same time  so the pool package is meant to solve  this problem by creating a pool of  connections and it's so expressive the  connections from the remote server and  then it keeps them locally and you can  check them out as you need and return  them and it do